export const DATA_80: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-119", num: 119, title: "Đề 119", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Samurai: Origins, Way of Life, and Enduring Cultural Legacy", paragraphs: ["Few historical figures have captured the global imagination as durably as the samurai, the warrior class that dominated Japanese society for nearly seven centuries. The word \"samurai\" derives from the Old Japanese verb saburau, meaning \"to serve\" or \"to attend,\" and this etymology is significant: the samurai were not, at their origins, an independent military caste pursuing their own interests but a class of armed retainers in service to the aristocratic clans that controlled land and political authority in pre-medieval Japan. Their emergence as a distinct social class is generally traced to the late Heian period of the tenth and eleventh centuries, when the imperial court at Kyoto found itself increasingly dependent on provincial warrior clans — most notably the Minamoto and the Taira — to suppress regional rebellions and maintain order in territories beyond the capital's direct administrative reach.", "The establishment of the first samurai-led government — the Kamakura Shogunate, founded by Minamoto no Yoritomo in 1185 — marked a decisive shift in Japanese political organization. Power migrated from the imperial court to the military class, initiating a period of warrior governance that would persist, through successive shogunates, until the Meiji Restoration of 1868. During these nearly seven centuries of samurai political dominance, the warrior class underwent profound transformations: from relatively rough provincial fighters concerned primarily with battlefield effectiveness, they evolved into a cultivated elite whose identity encompassed not only martial skill but also literary accomplishment, aesthetic sensibility, administrative capacity, and a codified system of ethical conduct.", "That ethical system — known as bushido, or \"the way of the warrior\" — is perhaps the most widely recognized and most frequently misunderstood aspect of samurai culture. The term bushido itself was not codified until the Edo period (1603–1868), during which prolonged internal peace paradoxically intensified the samurai class's concern with articulating the values that distinguished them from other social groups. The core principles most consistently associated with bushido include loyalty to one's lord, personal honor, martial courage, self-discipline, and a particular attitude toward death — encapsulated in the famous dictum that a samurai should live each moment as though he were already dead, thereby freeing himself from the fear that would otherwise compromise his effectiveness in service. The practice of seppuku, ritual self-disembowelment performed to avoid capture or to atone for failure or dishonor, represented the most extreme expression of these values and served simultaneously as a mark of individual dignity and an assertion of the samurai's ultimate autonomy over his own fate.", "The daily life of a samurai was shaped by the hierarchical relationships — between lord and retainer, master and apprentice — that structured Edo-period society. A samurai's primary obligation was to his daimyo, the feudal lord whose domain he served, and the relationship was understood as one of absolute fidelity: a samurai who survived the death of his lord without having avenged him, or who abandoned his service for personal advantage, was regarded with deep social contempt. The famous tale of the Forty-Seven Ronin — samurai who spent years planning and ultimately executing revenge for their master's death before taking their own lives — became one of the defining moral narratives of the Edo period precisely because it dramatized the ideal of loyalty carried to its ultimate conclusion. Beyond these formal obligations, samurai households were expected to cultivate refinement: calligraphy, poetry composition, the tea ceremony, and the appreciation of No theater were all considered appropriate pursuits for a warrior class that increasingly defined itself through cultural as well as martial distinction.", "The Meiji Restoration of 1868 abolished the formal samurai class and dismantled the feudal political structure that had sustained it for centuries. The new Meiji government, committed to rapid modernization along Western lines, disbanded samurai domains, replaced the traditional military with a conscript army, and withdrew the legal privileges — including the exclusive right to carry swords — that had defined samurai social status. Many former samurai found productive roles in the new Meiji state as military officers, government bureaucrats, educators, and industrialists, but the class as a legally recognized entity ceased to exist. The Satsuma Rebellion of 1877, in which a traditionalist samurai faction led by Saigo Takamori rose against the Meiji government before being crushed by the new conscript army, marked the final armed resistance of the samurai class to its own dissolution.", "Despite — or perhaps because of — its historical termination, samurai culture has exercised a remarkable influence on Japanese national identity and, through Japanese cultural exports, on global popular culture. Within Japan, the values associated with bushido were consciously revived and redeployed by Meiji-era nationalists seeking to construct a coherent national character capable of sustaining the social discipline required by rapid industrialization. This revival was not simply nostalgic but politically purposeful: the loyalty, self-sacrifice, and martial spirit attributed to the samurai were reframed as quintessentially Japanese virtues that the nation as a whole was called upon to embody. The results were profound and, in some cases, deeply troubling: the same value system that celebrated self-sacrifice and absolute loyalty to authority contributed to the militaristic nationalism that propelled Japan into the catastrophic conflicts of the twentieth century.", "In contemporary global culture, the samurai image has undergone a further transformation — stripped of much of its specific historical context and refracted through cinema, manga, anime, video games, and martial arts practice into an archetype of disciplined, honorable, aesthetically refined martial excellence. Films such as Akira Kurosawa's Seven Samurai have influenced filmmakers across the world, and the conceptual vocabulary of bushido has been widely adopted — often loosely and selectively — in motivational literature, business management theory, and competitive sports culture. The actual historical samurai — products of a specific feudal society, defined by specific hierarchical obligations, and capable of considerable cruelty as well as remarkable elegance — are in many respects barely recognizable in the idealized global figure they have become. Yet the persistence and global reach of the samurai archetype testify to the enduring human appeal of a figure who appears to have reconciled, however imperfectly, the demands of violence and beauty, duty and self-mastery."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To argue that the global image of the samurai has been so distorted by popular culture that it no longer bears any meaningful relationship to the historical reality of the warrior class", "To trace the samurai from their origins as armed retainers through their cultural development, political decline, and enduring influence on Japanese and global culture", "To compare the political power of different samurai shogunates and assess which period of warrior governance had the most lasting impact on Japanese social organization", "To evaluate whether the revival of bushido values during the Meiji period was a positive or negative development for Japanese national identity and social cohesion"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the etymology of the word \"samurai\" reveal about the class's original social role?", options: ["It reveals that samurai were armed retainers in service to others, indicating that their original identity was defined by loyal service rather than independent military or political power", "It reveals that samurai were primarily ceremonial figures attached to the imperial court, whose martial function was secondary to their role in maintaining aristocratic ritual", "It reveals that samurai were originally a hereditary aristocratic caste who possessed independent political authority over provincial territories from the earliest period of their existence", "It reveals that samurai considered military service a spiritual vocation, which explains why their ethical code later developed such a strong emphasis on self-sacrifice and death"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what dual significance did the practice of seppuku carry within samurai culture?", options: ["It served as both a military tactic for avoiding useful information falling into enemy hands and a public demonstration of a clan's collective commitment to the values of bushido", "It served both as a punishment imposed by a lord on a samurai who had failed in duty and as a voluntary act of protest against decisions considered unjust by the warrior class", "It functioned simultaneously as a mark of individual dignity and an assertion of the samurai's ultimate autonomy over his own fate when facing capture or dishonor", "It represented both a personal act of religious devotion and a formal legal procedure by which a samurai could transfer his obligations to a designated successor"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did the tale of the Forty-Seven Ronin become a defining moral narrative of the Edo period?", options: ["Because it demonstrated that samurai could successfully challenge the authority of the shogunate when acting collectively in defense of a universally recognized principle of justice", "Because it showed that samurai who had lost their lord could rebuild honorable lives through discipline and cultural refinement rather than through continued pursuit of military service", "Because it illustrated the conflict between personal loyalty to an individual lord and the broader obligations that samurai owed to the social order of the Edo shogunate", "Because it dramatized the ideal of loyalty carried to its ultimate conclusion — years of patient planning, successful revenge, and final self-sacrifice — which embodied the highest samurai values"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The successive shogunates that governed Japan from the establishment of the Kamakura Shogunate in 1185 through to the Meiji Restoration of 1868", "The profound transformations undergone by the samurai class as it evolved from rough provincial fighters into a cultivated elite with administrative and literary accomplishments", "The provincial warrior clans — most notably the Minamoto and the Taira — whose military power enabled them to displace the imperial court's political authority", "The nearly seven centuries during which samurai-led governments held political power in Japan, spanning from the late twelfth century to the mid-nineteenth century"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["The Meiji Restoration of 1868, which the feudal political system had been unable to prevent despite the efforts of conservative samurai factions to resist Western-influenced modernization", "The formal samurai class as a legally recognized social entity, which the feudal political structure had sustained through the legal privileges and hierarchical obligations it enforced", "The feudal domain system of the Edo period, which the Meiji government abolished as part of its program of rapid centralization and modernization along Western lines", "The traditional right to carry swords, which had defined samurai social status and which the feudal political structure had protected as an exclusive privilege of the warrior class"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"quintessentially\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Historically, meaning that the values in question were attributed to the samurai on the basis of careful scholarly documentation of their actual conduct across the feudal period", "Surprisingly, meaning that the association of loyalty and self-sacrifice with Japanese national character was unexpected given the samurai class's relatively recent political dominance", "In the most pure and representative way possible, meaning that these values were presented as the most essential and characteristic expression of Japanese national identity", "Controversially, meaning that the identification of bushido values as Japanese national virtues was disputed by many intellectuals and reformers of the Meiji period"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"refracted\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Transformed as it passes through different media or perspectives, taking on new forms and altered meanings in the process of cultural transmission", "Deliberately distorted, meaning that filmmakers and game designers have consciously misrepresented samurai history to make their products more commercially appealing", "Gradually forgotten, meaning that the original historical significance of samurai culture has been progressively lost as popular representations have replaced scholarly ones", "Preserved accurately, meaning that the samurai image has been transmitted through cinema and popular culture in a form that faithfully represents its historical origins"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cultural pursuit expected of samurai households during the Edo period?", options: ["The practice of calligraphy and the composition of poetry as marks of literary cultivation", "The performance of and participation in the tea ceremony as a refined social ritual", "The appreciation of No theater as an appropriate cultural pursuit for the warrior class", "The study of architectural design and landscape gardening as expressions of aesthetic sensibility"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the relationship between the historical samurai and the global archetype they have become?", options: ["The transformation of the samurai into a global cultural archetype has been driven primarily by Japanese government efforts to promote a positive national image through cultural export strategies", "The global samurai archetype is essentially accurate because cinema and popular culture have drawn primarily on scholarly historical research rather than on romantic or nationalistic interpretations", "The global popularity of the samurai archetype reflects a universal recognition that the values of bushido — loyalty, honor, and self-discipline — are objectively superior to those of other warrior traditions", "The global samurai archetype selectively emphasizes idealized qualities while omitting the historical complexity, specific feudal context, and capacity for cruelty that characterized the actual warrior class"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Speech and Language Difficulties in Children: Identification, Causes, and Intervention", paragraphs: ["Language is the primary medium through which children participate in social life, acquire knowledge, and construct their identities as learners. For the majority of children, the journey from the first babbled syllables of infancy to the complex, context-sensitive language use of middle childhood unfolds with remarkable reliability, guided by a combination of biological maturation and environmental exposure that most developmental scientists regard as one of the most impressive achievements of the human organism. For a significant minority, however, this journey is disrupted — by difficulties that range from mild and transient delays in expressive vocabulary to severe and persistent disorders affecting the ability to produce, process, or discriminate the sounds, words, and grammatical structures on which meaningful communication depends. Understanding the nature of these difficulties, their causes, and the interventions most likely to support children who experience them has become one of the central concerns of developmental psychology, speech-language pathology, and early childhood education.", "Speech and language difficulties in children encompass a broad and heterogeneous range of conditions that researchers and clinicians distinguish with increasing precision. Speech sound disorders — difficulties in producing the phonemes, or distinct sound units, of a language correctly and consistently — represent one of the most common categories, affecting an estimated 2 to 13 percent of preschool-age children depending on the diagnostic criteria applied. Children with speech sound disorders may substitute one phoneme for another (saying \"wabbit\" for \"rabbit\"), omit sounds from words, or distort sounds in ways that make their speech difficult to understand for unfamiliar listeners. Separate from these production difficulties are language disorders, which affect the child's ability to comprehend or formulate language at the level of words, sentences, or connected discourse. A child with a receptive language disorder may struggle to follow multi-step instructions, understand complex sentence structures, or differentiate between words that are phonologically similar — meaning words that sound alike but carry distinct meanings, such as \"pin\" and \"pen\" or \"cat\" and \"cap.\"", "The causes of speech and language difficulties in children are diverse and in many cases interact in complex ways that resist simple categorization. Genetic factors play a significant role: family history of speech or language difficulties substantially increases a child's risk, and molecular genetic research has identified several gene variants — including mutations in the FOXP2 gene, the first gene definitively linked to a human speech and language disorder — that contribute to specific patterns of impairment. Neurological factors, including prenatal brain development disruptions, perinatal hypoxia, and postnatal acquired brain injury, can affect the cortical and subcortical regions involved in speech motor control, phonological processing, and language comprehension. Hearing loss is among the most consequential modifiable risk factors: even mild, fluctuating hearing loss associated with recurrent otitis media (middle ear infection) during the critical period of language acquisition can meaningfully disrupt the child's exposure to the acoustic input necessary for the development of phonological discrimination and vocabulary.", "The social and environmental context in which a child develops also exerts considerable influence on language outcomes. Children raised in environments characterized by rich, varied, and responsive verbal interaction with caregivers develop larger vocabularies, more complex syntactic structures, and stronger phonological awareness than those whose early language environments are impoverished in quantity or quality of adult speech. The Hart and Risley study of the 1990s famously documented a \"word gap\" of an estimated 30 million words by age three between children raised in professional households and those raised in families receiving welfare support — a finding that has been subsequently debated in its specific magnitude but whose core insight about the role of early language environment in shaping developmental outcomes has been consistently supported. These environmental disparities do not operate independently of biological vulnerability: children with genetic or neurological risk factors for language difficulties are likely to be more sensitive to the quality of their early language environment, a phenomenon described in developmental science as differential susceptibility.", "Identification of speech and language difficulties as early as possible is widely regarded as essential to effective intervention, given the well-established finding that the neural plasticity supporting language learning is greatest in the earliest years of life and diminishes progressively through childhood and into adolescence. Several screening tools have been developed for use in primary care and early childhood settings, including the Ages and Stages Questionnaire (ASQ) and the Communication and Symbolic Behavior Scales (CSBS), which allow clinicians and educators to identify children whose language development falls below expected milestones and who may benefit from more detailed evaluation by a qualified speech-language pathologist. Early identification does not, however, always translate smoothly into timely access to services: waiting lists for publicly funded speech-language therapy can extend to many months in numerous health systems, and the availability of specialist services in rural and low-income communities remains substantially inferior to that in urban and higher-income areas.", "Speech-language therapy for children with phonological difficulties typically employs a range of approaches targeting the child's ability to perceive, discriminate, and produce speech sounds accurately. Minimal pair therapy — in which the child is presented with pairs of words differing in a single phoneme (such as \"bear\" and \"pear\") and trained to recognize and produce the distinction between them — has accumulated substantial evidence of effectiveness for children with phonological disorders and is among the most widely used clinical techniques. Naturalistic approaches, including milieu teaching and the Hanen \"It Takes Two to Talk\" program, embed language facilitation within everyday communicative interactions rather than formal drill, and are particularly well-suited for younger children and those with broader language difficulties. For children with concomitant hearing loss, fitting of appropriately programmed hearing aids or cochlear implantation, combined with intensive auditory-verbal therapy, can produce remarkable gains in speech and language development that significantly narrow the gap between these children and their typically developing peers.", "The longer-term consequences of unaddressed or inadequately treated speech and language difficulties extend well beyond the communicative domain. Longitudinal research has consistently found that children with significant language difficulties at school entry are at elevated risk for reading and literacy difficulties, academic underachievement, social isolation, and mental health problems including anxiety and depression in adolescence and adulthood. The relationship between oral language and literacy is particularly robust: phonological awareness — the ability to identify and manipulate the sound structure of spoken words — is among the strongest predictors of early reading acquisition, and children whose phonological processing is impaired are at substantially elevated risk for developmental dyslexia. Early and effective intervention for speech and language difficulties may therefore have benefits that cascade well beyond speech itself, contributing to outcomes in literacy, academic achievement, and long-term social and emotional functioning."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To evaluate the long-term academic and social consequences of childhood language difficulties and propose policy reforms to improve access to speech-language services", "To argue that genetic factors are the primary determinant of speech and language difficulties and that environmental interventions have only limited effectiveness in addressing them", "To examine the range of speech and language difficulties in children, their varied causes, the importance of early identification, and the interventions available to support affected children", "To compare the effectiveness of different speech-language therapy approaches and recommend which methods should be prioritized by clinicians working with young children"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following most accurately describes the ways in which speech sound disorders can affect a child's spoken language?", options: ["Children experience difficulty producing words of more than two syllables but can accurately produce all individual phonemes in isolation when prompted by a trained clinician", "Children with speech sound disorders primarily struggle with understanding complex sentences and following multi-step instructions delivered at normal conversational speed", "Children may substitute one phoneme for another, omit sounds from words, or distort sounds in ways that make their speech difficult for unfamiliar listeners to understand", "Children with speech sound disorders typically have difficulty with reading and writing but produce spoken language that is fully intelligible to both familiar and unfamiliar listeners"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what characterizes a receptive language disorder in a child?", options: ["The child understands spoken language at an age-appropriate level but experiences significant difficulty formulating and expressing ideas in grammatically correct spoken sentences", "The child struggles to comprehend language, including difficulty following multi-step instructions, understanding complex sentences, and distinguishing phonologically similar words", "The child's vocabulary development is significantly delayed but sentence structure and phonological production remain within the expected range for the child's age", "The child produces speech sounds incorrectly and is unable to self-monitor or correct errors even when provided with immediate feedback from an adult or peer"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the significance of the FOXP2 gene in the study of childhood speech and language disorders?", options: ["It encodes the protein responsible for producing myelin in the neural pathways connecting Broca's area to the motor cortex, and its mutations slow speech motor development", "It is the gene responsible for regulating the growth of the auditory cortex, and mutations in it have been linked to the hearing loss that frequently underlies speech sound disorders", "It was the first gene definitively linked to a human speech and language disorder, establishing a clear genetic contribution to specific patterns of speech and language impairment", "It is the most commonly mutated gene in children with language disorders, accounting for the majority of cases in which no environmental or neurological cause can be identified"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does minimal pair therapy support children with phonological difficulties?", options: ["It presents children with pairs of words differing in a single phoneme and trains them to recognize and produce the distinction between those sounds", "It combines intensive parent training with structured home practice sessions to ensure that phonological targets introduced in therapy are generalized to the child's natural environment", "It embeds phonological training within naturally occurring communicative interactions so that children practice sound distinctions in meaningful everyday contexts rather than formal drill", "It uses digital audio equipment to amplify specific phonemic contrasts so that children with mild hearing loss can perceive distinctions they would otherwise miss"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["The findings of the Hart and Risley study, including the estimated 30-million-word gap between children from different household types by the age of three", "The genetic and neurological risk factors discussed earlier in the passage that make certain children more vulnerable to speech and language difficulties regardless of their environment", "The environmental disparities in the quantity and quality of early language input that have been documented between children from different socioeconomic backgrounds", "The developmental outcomes — including vocabulary size, syntactic complexity, and phonological awareness — that are shaped by the quality of a child's early language environment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["The speech-language pathologists who administer minimal pair therapy and assess the child's ability to perceive and produce phonological distinctions accurately", "The phonemes that differ between word pairs, which the child must learn to perceive and articulate distinctly in order to communicate meaning reliably to listeners", "The pairs of minimally different words — such as \"bear\" and \"pear\" — that the child is presented with and trained to recognize and produce as distinct from one another", "The children with phonological disorders who are enrolled in minimal pair therapy and trained to produce speech sounds accurately in both structured and naturalistic settings"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"necessary\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Specialized, meaning that the acoustic input must be delivered by trained caregivers using specific techniques designed to optimize phonological development in young children", "Stimulating, meaning that the acoustic input must be sufficiently varied and engaging to maintain a young child's attention during the critical period of language acquisition", "Abundant, meaning that children require an unusually large quantity of acoustic input to develop phonological discrimination and vocabulary within the expected developmental timeline", "Required or essential, meaning that without adequate acoustic input the normal development of phonological discrimination and vocabulary cannot take place"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"concomitant\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Severe, meaning that the hearing loss experienced by these children is of a degree that prevents them from benefiting from conventional hearing aid amplification alone", "Progressive, meaning that these children's hearing loss has been developing gradually over time rather than being present from birth or early infancy", "Undiagnosed, meaning that the hearing loss in these children has not yet been identified and is therefore contributing to their speech difficulties without clinical awareness", "Occurring at the same time or alongside something else, meaning that these children have hearing loss in addition to their speech and language difficulties"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a potential long-term consequence of unaddressed speech and language difficulties in children?", options: ["Difficulties with reading and literacy development, linked to impaired phonological awareness which is a strong predictor of early reading acquisition", "Academic underachievement at school, resulting from the cascading effects of language difficulties on learning across multiple subject areas", "Social isolation and mental health problems including anxiety and depression in adolescence and adulthood", "Delayed physical development and motor coordination difficulties that persist from early childhood into the teenage years"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Evolution of Social Media: Platforms, Power, and the Transformation of Public Life", paragraphs: ["The phrase \"social media\" has become so embedded in everyday language that it is easy to forget how recent the phenomenon it describes actually is. The platforms that now mediate significant proportions of human communication, political participation, commercial activity, and cultural production — Facebook, YouTube, Twitter, Instagram, TikTok, and their counterparts — did not exist before the early 2000s, and the forms of social interaction they have made possible bear only a partial resemblance to anything that preceded them. Yet the transformations social media has produced in public life have been so rapid and so pervasive that the scholarly literature attempting to account for them has struggled to keep pace. Understanding social media's evolution — how these platforms developed, what structural features drive their behavior, and what consequences they have produced for the societies in which they operate — requires engaging simultaneously with the history of technology, the economics of digital platforms, and the sociology of communication.", "The genealogy of social media is typically traced to a cluster of early platforms developed in the late 1990s and early 2000s — SixDegrees, Friendster, and MySpace — that established the basic template of the profile-based social network: a persistent personal page, a visible list of connections, and the ability to share content with those connections. These early platforms demonstrated that there was substantial user appetite for digitally mediated social connection, but they also revealed the limitations of models that could not scale effectively, maintain user trust, or evolve their feature sets in response to changing user behavior. Facebook, launched in 2004, succeeded where earlier platforms had struggled in part because of its initial restriction to university networks — a design choice that created a perception of exclusivity and social relevance — and in part because of its early development of features such as the News Feed (introduced in 2006) that made social information flows continuous, personalized, and highly visible.", "The introduction of the smartphone as the primary interface for digital communication, accelerated by the launch of the iPhone in 2007 and the subsequent proliferation of Android devices, transformed social media from a desktop-based activity into a persistent, ambient presence in users' lives. Mobile connectivity dissolved the temporal and spatial boundaries that had previously structured media consumption: social media became something that users carried with them at all times, consulted in every interstitial moment, and integrated into the fabric of daily experience in ways that no previous communication medium had managed. This shift fundamentally altered the economics of social media platforms, which discovered that the combination of continuous mobile access and algorithmically curated feeds could generate levels of user engagement — measured in daily active users, session frequency, and time spent on platform — that had no precedent in the history of media.", "The business model that emerged from this discovery was built on the commodification of user attention. Social media platforms, offered free to users, generate revenue by selling advertisers access to audiences whose demographic characteristics, behavioral patterns, and inferred preferences have been profiled with a precision previously unavailable to the advertising industry. The data generated by users through their interactions — posts, likes, shares, search queries, and the patterns of content consumption revealed by scrolling behavior — constitute the raw material from which this profiling is constructed. [1] This model created powerful incentives for platform designers to maximize engagement above all other objectives, since engagement time is the primary determinant of advertising inventory and therefore of revenue. The consequences of optimizing for engagement at the expense of other values — including informational accuracy, emotional wellbeing, and civic health — have become one of the defining controversies of the digital age.", "Social media's impact on political life has been among the most intensely debated dimensions of its evolution. The platforms' capacity to enable rapid, low-cost mass communication gave significant organizational advantages to political movements, campaigns, and actors operating outside established institutional channels, a dynamic that contributed to movements as varied as the Arab Spring of 2010–2011, the Occupy movement, the Black Lives Matter movement, and the political campaigns of figures from Barack Obama to Donald Trump. Yet the same structural features that enabled these forms of political mobilization — algorithmic amplification of emotionally engaging content, network effects that concentrate attention on a small number of highly shared messages, and the removal of traditional gatekeeping functions of professional journalism — also created conditions conducive to the rapid spread of misinformation, the intensification of political polarization, and the exploitation of social media infrastructure by state and non-state actors pursuing anti-democratic objectives. [2]", "The economic concentration of social media into a small number of extraordinarily powerful global platforms has raised fundamental questions about market competition, democratic governance, and the appropriate regulatory relationship between states and digital intermediaries. By 2020, Meta (formerly Facebook), Alphabet (parent company of Google and YouTube), and ByteDance (owner of TikTok) collectively commanded a share of global digital advertising revenue and user attention that would have triggered immediate regulatory intervention in any conventional product market. [3] The regulatory responses that have emerged — including the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the Digital Markets Act, and various national legislative initiatives addressing content moderation and algorithmic transparency — reflect a growing recognition that the laissez-faire approach to platform governance that characterized the early social media era is no longer sustainable, without yet producing a coherent international framework capable of matching the global scale at which these platforms operate.", "The most recent phase of social media evolution has been characterized by the emergence of short-form video as the dominant content format, driven largely by TikTok's algorithmic model, which differs from earlier social media in a structurally significant way: where Facebook and Instagram organized content primarily around social graphs — the network of connections between users — TikTok's recommendation system delivers content based primarily on inferred interest rather than social connection, making content discovery radically independent of a user's existing social network. [4] This interest-graph model has proven extraordinarily effective at generating engagement and has been rapidly adopted, in modified form, by competitor platforms including YouTube Shorts and Instagram Reels, suggesting that the interest-graph approach may represent a structural shift in the architecture of social media rather than merely a temporary trend driven by a single platform's market position."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of this passage?", options: ["To evaluate the adequacy of current regulatory frameworks in addressing the concentration of economic and political power in a small number of global social media platforms", "To compare the social graph model used by Facebook and Instagram with the interest-graph model pioneered by TikTok and evaluate which approach better serves users' informational needs", "To trace the evolution of social media from its early platforms through its structural transformation by mobile technology, its business model, its political consequences, and its most recent architectural shifts", "To argue that social media platforms have been net harmful to democratic societies and that their growth should be constrained through urgent international regulatory intervention"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The limitations that prevented early social media platforms from scaling effectively, maintaining user trust, and evolving their features in response to changing user behavior", "The structural features — profile pages, visible connection lists, and content sharing — that defined the basic template of the profile-based social network from its earliest iterations", "The early social media platforms of the late 1990s and early 2000s, specifically SixDegrees, Friendster, and MySpace, which established the foundational social network model", "The universities whose networks Facebook initially restricted itself to, creating the perception of exclusivity that contributed to its early success over predecessor platforms"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"curated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Deliberately selected and organized according to specific criteria, meaning that algorithmic systems actively shape what content is presented to each individual user", "Randomly generated, meaning that the content appearing in users' feeds is selected by an automated system without any consideration of individual behavioral patterns or preferences", "Chronologically ordered, meaning that content appears in the sequence in which it was posted rather than being prioritized according to any engagement-maximizing logic", "Collaboratively produced, meaning that the content in social media feeds is assembled through the collective decisions of a user's network connections rather than by algorithmic selection"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"interstitial\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Occurring in the small gaps or in-between moments of daily life, meaning the brief pauses between other activities when users check their social media feeds", "Planned and deliberate, meaning the specific scheduled times that users set aside in their daily routines to engage with social media content on their mobile devices", "Emotionally significant, meaning the personally meaningful moments during the day when users are most likely to share content or engage with others on social platforms", "Late-night, meaning the hours after conventional working and social commitments have ended when users are most likely to engage with social media without interruption"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which two factors are identified as contributing to Facebook's success over earlier social media platforms?", options: ["Its decision to allow users to maintain anonymous profiles rather than real identities, and its early introduction of advertising tools that generated revenue without displaying intrusive advertisements", "Its superior privacy protections compared to MySpace and Friendster, and its early development of a mobile application that allowed users to access the platform from any location", "Its initial restriction to university networks, which created a sense of exclusivity, and its development of the News Feed in 2006, which made social information flows continuous and personalized", "Its acquisition of competing platforms before they could attract significant user bases, and its investment in content moderation systems that made the platform safer than its predecessors"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what types of user-generated data do social media platforms use to construct advertising profiles?", options: ["Device usage patterns, including the apps installed on users' phones and the frequency with which they switch between applications during a single mobile session", "Demographic information voluntarily provided at account registration, including age, location, and stated interests, which platforms cross-reference with external consumer databases", "Posts, likes, shares, search queries, and patterns of content consumption revealed by scrolling behavior, which collectively constitute the raw material for user profiling", "Direct messages and private communications between users, which platforms analyze using natural language processing to infer emotional states and purchasing intentions"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the key structural difference between TikTok's content recommendation model and those of Facebook and Instagram?", options: ["TikTok's algorithm prioritizes content from users' geographic region, while Facebook and Instagram recommend content based on the global popularity of posts across all markets", "TikTok uses human editorial teams to select trending content for users, while Facebook and Instagram rely exclusively on automated algorithmic systems with no human oversight", "TikTok restricts its recommendation system to content produced by verified creators, while Facebook and Instagram recommend content from any user regardless of account status", "TikTok recommends content based primarily on inferred interest rather than social connection, while Facebook and Instagram organized content primarily around users' existing social networks"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an example of social media's influence on political life?", options: ["The Brexit referendum of 2016, in which social media was used by both campaign sides to target voters with personalized political messaging at unprecedented scale", "The Arab Spring of 2010–2011, in which social media platforms contributed to the organizational capacity of political movements across the Middle East and North Africa", "The Black Lives Matter movement, which used social media's capacity for rapid low-cost mass communication to mobilize support outside established institutional channels", "The political campaigns of figures including Barack Obama and Donald Trump, which exploited social media's structural features to reach and mobilize voters"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Critics have argued that this arrangement constitutes a fundamental inversion of the traditional media relationship, in which audiences paid for content and were therefore the customers rather than the product.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the likely future direction of social media platform architecture?", options: ["Social media platforms will likely return to social-graph models as users become fatigued by algorithmically recommended content and express preferences for content from people they already know", "The interest-graph model pioneered by TikTok may represent an enduring architectural shift across social media rather than a temporary trend, given that major competitor platforms have already adopted versions of it", "Short-form video will eventually be replaced by a new content format not yet developed, as the rapid historical progression from text to images to video suggests the cycle will continue accelerating", "Regulatory pressure on TikTok's data practices will likely force the platform to adopt a social-graph model similar to Facebook's, reducing the architectural divergence between major platforms"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Incarceration, Crime, and the Limits of Punitive Justice", paragraphs: ["The relationship between imprisonment and crime reduction is one of the most consequential and least resolved questions in contemporary criminal justice policy. Most democratic societies operate on the implicit assumption that incarceration serves simultaneously as a mechanism of incapacitation — removing individuals who have committed crimes from the environments in which they can reoffend — and as a deterrent, signaling to potential offenders that criminal behavior will be met with punishment sufficiently costly to outweigh its anticipated benefits. These twin justifications have sustained the dramatic expansion of prison populations across many countries over the past half-century. Yet the empirical evidence supporting them is considerably more ambiguous than the political rhetoric surrounding criminal justice policy typically acknowledges, and the social costs of mass incarceration — concentrated disproportionately in already-disadvantaged communities — have prompted a growing body of scholars, policymakers, and practitioners to question whether the prison, as currently constituted, represents a rational or effective response to the problem of crime.", "The scale of incarceration in the United States provides the most extreme illustration of the punitive turn in modern criminal justice. The United States incarcerates approximately 2 million people at any given time — the highest absolute and per-capita prison population of any nation in the world — a figure that represents a roughly fivefold increase from levels recorded in the early 1970s. This expansion occurred not as a consequence of a corresponding increase in crime but largely as a result of deliberate policy choices: the mandatory minimum sentencing laws introduced during the War on Drugs era, truth-in-sentencing requirements that reduced parole eligibility, and three-strikes legislation that imposed severe sentences for repeat offenders regardless of the nature of the triggering offense. The racial disparities embedded in these policies have been extensively documented: African American men are incarcerated at approximately five times the rate of white men, a disparity that reflects both differential enforcement of drug laws and the cumulative effects of socioeconomic disadvantages that leave some communities more exposed to the conditions that produce criminal involvement.", "The empirical literature on the relationship between incarceration rates and crime rates does not support a straightforward deterrence narrative. Research has consistently found that the certainty of punishment — the probability that a crime will result in arrest and conviction — has a stronger deterrent effect than the severity of punishment, meaning that increasing sentence lengths produces diminishing returns in terms of crime reduction. Studies examining the crime-reduction effects of the US prison expansion of the 1980s and 1990s have generally found modest incapacitation benefits — the removal of active offenders from crime-producing environments does reduce crime to some degree — but have also found that these benefits are offset by criminogenic effects of incarceration itself: imprisonment disrupts employment, family relationships, and community ties in ways that increase the probability of reoffending after release.", "The criminogenic effects of incarceration are particularly pronounced for young offenders and for individuals imprisoned for nonviolent offenses. Exposure to the social environment of prison — characterized by high concentrations of individuals with criminal histories, limited access to educational or vocational programming, and institutional cultures that reward aggressive posturing and dominance — can solidify criminal identities, expand criminal networks, and degrade the prosocial skills and attachments that function as protective factors against continued offending. Recidivism rates in most developed countries remain stubbornly high: approximately two-thirds of released prisoners in the United States are rearrested within three years of release, a figure that has remained largely stable despite decades of policy experimentation, suggesting that the prison environment itself — rather than the failure of any particular reintegration program — may be the principal driver of continued criminal involvement.", "Alternative approaches to criminal justice have produced evidence of more promising outcomes in several national contexts. Norway's prison system — among the most frequently cited international comparisons — operates on an explicitly rehabilitative model: facilities are designed to approximate normal living conditions as closely as possible, prisoners have access to education, vocational training, and therapeutic programs, and the explicit institutional goal is the preparation of prisoners for successful community reintegration rather than punishment as an end in itself. Norway's recidivism rate of approximately 20 percent — compared to 66 percent in the United States — is regularly invoked as evidence for the rehabilitative model's superiority, though researchers caution that direct comparisons are complicated by substantial differences in social inequality, drug policy, and the demographic composition of prison populations between the two countries. [1]", "Restorative justice — an approach that prioritizes repairing the harm caused by criminal behavior through structured dialogue between offenders, victims, and affected community members — has emerged as another significant alternative to purely punitive responses. [2] Randomized controlled trials and systematic reviews of restorative justice programs have generally found positive effects on victim satisfaction, offender accountability, and reoffending rates compared to conventional court processing, with particularly strong evidence for programs targeting juvenile offenders and non-serious adult offenses. The theoretical rationale for these effects draws on criminological research demonstrating that shame reintegration — the experience of confronting the human consequences of one's actions within a context of community support rather than social exclusion — is more effective at producing genuine behavioral change than the stigmatizing shame associated with formal criminal conviction and imprisonment.", "The political economy of criminal justice reform presents formidable obstacles to the adoption of evidence-based alternatives to incarceration. Prison systems represent significant economic interests — correctional officer unions, private prison corporations, and the communities that depend on prison employment — whose institutional incentives are aligned with maintaining or expanding incarceration rather than reducing it. [3] Public attitudes toward criminal justice, shaped by decades of politically motivated tough-on-crime rhetoric and by the selective emphasis on violent crime in media coverage, have historically favored punitive responses even in the face of evidence that they are less effective than alternatives. The victims of crime — whose experiences and needs are systematically instrumentalized in tough-on-crime political narratives — frequently express, when surveyed outside political contexts, preferences for restitution, accountability, and community safety rather than for the extended imprisonment of offenders. [4]", "What the accumulated evidence on incarceration and crime ultimately suggests is that the prison, as deployed in most contemporary criminal justice systems, reflects the political logic of punishment — its visibility, its apparent decisiveness, its capacity to signal that wrongdoing will be met with consequences — more faithfully than it reflects the empirical logic of crime reduction. Reforming criminal justice in ways that better align institutional practice with evidence about what actually reduces crime and repairs communities will require confronting not only the economic and political interests that sustain the current system but the deeper cultural assumptions about punishment, accountability, and human capacity for change that make incarceration seem, to many, not merely effective but morally necessary."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best represents the central argument of the passage?", options: ["That the United States prison system is uniquely dysfunctional and that other countries should avoid adopting its mandatory sentencing and truth-in-sentencing policies", "That racial disparities in incarceration rates are the most urgent problem in criminal justice and that addressing them should take precedence over debates about the effectiveness of imprisonment", "That incarceration as currently practiced reflects political rather than empirical logic, producing social costs and recidivism rates that alternative approaches have demonstrated can be significantly reduced", "That restorative justice represents the single most effective alternative to incarceration and should be adopted universally to replace conventional court processing for all categories of offense"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"solidify\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To conceal strategically, meaning that offenders learn to hide their criminal identities within prison in order to secure early release and better treatment from correctional staff", "To undermine gradually, meaning that repeated exposure to the prison environment slowly erodes the criminal identities that offenders held prior to incarceration", "To make firmer and more established, meaning that the prison environment reinforces and entrenches criminal identities rather than weakening or transforming them", "To publicly display, meaning that the prison environment provides offenders with opportunities to demonstrate their criminal credentials to peers and potential associates"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The concept of \"shame reintegration\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A therapeutic technique in which offenders are required to revisit the scene of their crime in the presence of victims as a means of deepening their sense of remorse", "The experience of confronting the consequences of one's actions within a context of community support, producing behavioral change without the social exclusion of formal criminal conviction", "A judicial procedure in which offenders are publicly identified and shamed before their communities as a deterrent intended to prevent them from committing further offenses", "A reintegration model in which formerly incarcerated individuals are monitored by community members who report any signs of renewed criminal involvement to correctional authorities"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["The United States' position as the nation with the highest absolute and per-capita prison population in the world, which distinguishes it from all other democratic societies", "The introduction of mandatory minimum sentencing laws during the War on Drugs era, which altered the relationship between criminal conviction and the length of prison sentences", "The roughly fivefold expansion of the US prison population from levels recorded in the early 1970s to the approximately 2 million people currently incarcerated", "The racial disparity documented between African American and white male incarceration rates, which reflects differential enforcement of drug laws and cumulative socioeconomic disadvantages"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["The studies examining the crime-reduction effects of the US prison expansion of the 1980s and 1990s, which produced findings that complicate a straightforward deterrence narrative", "The modest incapacitation benefits produced by removing active offenders from crime-producing environments during the period of their incarceration", "The criminogenic effects of incarceration — including disruption to employment, family relationships, and community ties — that increase the probability of reoffending after release", "The diminishing returns of increasing sentence lengths as a deterrent strategy, which research has consistently identified as less effective than increasing the certainty of punishment"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does empirical research indicate about the relative deterrent effects of punishment certainty versus punishment severity?", options: ["Severity of punishment has a stronger deterrent effect than certainty, meaning that increasing sentence lengths is a more effective crime reduction strategy than improving arrest and conviction rates", "The deterrent effects of certainty and severity are equivalent for violent offenses but diverge significantly for property and drug offenses, where certainty is substantially more important", "Certainty of punishment has a stronger deterrent effect than severity, meaning that increasing the probability of arrest and conviction reduces crime more effectively than extending sentence lengths", "Both certainty and severity of punishment have negligible deterrent effects, meaning that neither increasing sentence lengths nor improving enforcement rates produces meaningful reductions in crime"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what caution do researchers express about comparing Norway's recidivism rate with that of the United States?", options: ["They caution that direct comparisons are complicated by substantial differences in social inequality, drug policy, and the demographic composition of prison populations between the two countries", "They caution that Norway's low recidivism rate is primarily attributable to its small population size rather than to the design of its prison system or its rehabilitative approach", "They caution that Norway's recidivism data is collected using different methodological definitions of reoffending that make it systematically lower than equivalent US figures", "They caution that Norway's rehabilitative model has only been in operation for a relatively short period and that its long-term effects on recidivism rates cannot yet be accurately assessed"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is identified as a structural economic interest aligned with maintaining or expanding incarceration?", options: ["Legal aid organizations that represent incarcerated individuals, whose funding levels are tied to the size of the prison population they are contracted to serve", "Technology firms that supply surveillance and monitoring equipment to corrections departments, whose contracts depend on continued high levels of prison admissions", "Correctional officer unions, private prison corporations, and communities that depend economically on prison employment as a significant local employer", "Insurance companies that provide coverage for property crimes, whose premium revenues increase proportionally with reported crime rates and prison population levels"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This gap between stated preferences and political outcomes suggests that criminal justice policy is driven less by the expressed needs of those most affected by crime than by the interests of those who benefit from the current system.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What does the author most likely suggest about the primary obstacle to evidence-based criminal justice reform?", options: ["The primary obstacle is the economic power of private prison corporations, whose lobbying influence over legislatures prevents the adoption of policies that would reduce prison populations", "The primary obstacle lies not only in economic and political interests but in deep cultural assumptions about punishment and human capacity for change that make incarceration feel morally necessary regardless of its empirical effectiveness", "The primary obstacle is public fear of violent crime, which makes it politically impossible for legislators to advocate for reduced incarceration even when they are personally persuaded by the evidence", "The primary obstacle is the absence of credible international evidence demonstrating that rehabilitative or restorative approaches can achieve lower recidivism rates than incarceration at comparable cost"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
];
